The {{nihongo|'''Tedorigawa Line'''|手取川線|Tedorigawa-sen}} is a [[rapid transit]] line operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Designated as '''Line 10''' of the Okami Metro network, it is identified by the color '''dark brown''' ({{HTML color|#663300}}) and carries the line code '''D'''. The line runs from {{nihongo|Nami-cho|波町}} in the southwest to {{nihongo|Hon-Karasaki|本唐崎}} in the northwest, spanning {{cvt|53.2|km|mi|0}} and serving 37 stations. With 37 stations across {{cvt|53.2|km|mi|abbr=on}}, it is one of the longest trunk lines in the Okami Metro system, behind only the [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]] (64 stations), [[Miyoshi Line]] (43 stations), and [[Nagata Line]] (38 stations).

The Tedorigawa Line is distinguished by its route through Okami's [[port]] and [[waterfront]] districts. The line passes through the Minato (port), Nagisa (waterfront), and Kuroshio (black tide) areas, serving as a critical transit link for the city's maritime industrial zones. The line's name derives from the Tedorigawa, a river that flows through the western portions of Okami, and the alignment roughly follows the river's course through several wards before curving northward through the port district.

The line offers interchanges with 13 other metro lines, making it one of the most connected lines in the network. Major transfer hubs include [[Nagata-cho (D)|Nagata-cho]] (with the [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] and [[Nankō Port Town Line]]), Kachidoki (with the [[Shitayanagi Line]] and [[Imaichi Line]]), and Tokushige (with the [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]]). The line's route through the Mitsu-Minato ward also provides connections to [[Okami-Tobu Airport]] via the [[Imaichi Line]].
==History==

===Planning and construction===

The Tedorigawa Line was conceived in the early 1970s as part of the fourth phase of expansion of the Okami Municipal Subway. The rapid growth of Okami's port and industrial areas following the post-war economic miracle created an urgent need for a transit line that could connect the waterfront districts with the city center and the growing residential suburbs to the northwest. The existing tram network in the port area was increasingly congested, and the narrow streets of the older port districts made surface-level transit expansion impractical.

Construction of the Tedorigawa Line began in 1976. The alignment was designed to serve the densely populated Minato and Mitsu-Minato wards, which housed much of the city's maritime workforce, as well as the growing residential areas of Kawauchi and Karasaki to the northwest. Unlike the [[Shitayanagi Line]], which had been built to narrow gauge, the Tedorigawa Line was constructed to [[standard gauge]] of {{RailGauge|sg|allk=on}}, consistent with the rest of the metro network.

===Opening and early operation===

The first segment of the Tedorigawa Line opened on July 1, 1981, between Nami-cho and Minato, covering approximately 25 kilometers and 23 stations. The opening was timed to coincide with the opening of the [[Nankō Port Town Line]] (New Tram), which together formed a comprehensive transit network for the port area. The Tedorigawa Line's opening was a major milestone for the port district, which had previously relied on buses and the aging tram system.

The second phase of construction extended the line northwest from Minato through the Mitsu-Minato ward to Hon-Karasaki, completing the full route in 1985. This extension brought the total line length to {{cvt|53.2|km|mi|0}} and added 14 new stations, serving the developing suburban areas of Karasaki and the Mitsu-Minato waterfront.

===Modernization===

In the 2000s, the Tedorigawa Line underwent significant modernization. The line received new signaling systems to increase service frequency, and several stations in the port district were renovated with improved accessibility features. The line's stations along the Nagisa and Kuroshio waterfront areas were redesigned to accommodate the growing number of commuters traveling to the newly developed commercial and residential complexes in the area.

Following the privatization of the [[Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau]] in 2018, the Tedorigawa Line received new 6000 series rolling stock manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]], replacing the aging 2000 series trains. The 2000 series fleet, which had been in service since the line's opening in 1981 and was refurbished in 2018, is being progressively phased out. As of 2026, the transition to the 6000 series is nearly complete, with full retirement of the 2000 series expected by 2027.

